course: everyday
lesson: 525
title: Friends who get it
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: After the Father's Day joke, Corey sits alone at lunch, picking at his chips. His mate Bilal plonks his tray down next to him. He doesn't make a speech. He just says, «My dad's a nightmare anyway, you're not missing much,» and nicks a chip. Corey laughs for the first time that day. Friends who get it don't need to fix anything. They turn up, sit down, and make it normal. Sienna-Rae is still red-eyed after «your own mum didn't want you» when Roisin says, «My cousin's in foster care. She says it's like having two families.» Sienna-Rae's shoulders drop. Knowing someone else is in the same boat helps.
explain: Good friends say the good bits out loud. Darius's buddy Tyler eats at his grandma's and says, «Your grandma makes the best mac and cheese, no cap» («no cap» = no lie). Darius grins all evening. Ffion's friend Megan says, «Mine split up too. Two Christmases, it's well lush» («lush» is Welsh slang for lovely). They laugh, and Ffion doesn't have to explain a thing. Jamal's mate Kieran says, «My uncle was inside» («inside» = in prison) «for two years. He's alright now, he coaches our footy.» Jamal feels less ashamed. Elijah's friend asks, «Can I come over? Your dads are so chill.» «Sure.» Aziza's friend Chloe says, «Your mum's scarf is so pretty, that colour's lush.» Aziza smiles.
explain: Some help is quiet. Holly-Mae's friend Jodie brings a jumper in a carrier bag. «Me mam said you can have it, it's too small for us.» No fuss, no audience. «Ta, pet,» says Holly-Mae («ta» = thanks, «pet» = love, in the North East). Liam-Jay's pal Ross says, «If ye ever need tae stay at mine, ma maw says it's fine» (Scots: ye = you, tae = to, ma maw = my mum). «Cheers, pal.» Liam-Jay doesn't need it that week, but he knows where to go. Kwesi's friend Tom invites him round for tea every Friday. «That boy's a good friend,» Kwesi's mum says, and she sends Tom home with a tub of her jollof rice.
explain: Some friends just see your family the way you do. Minnie's friend Ava says, «That's so cool, you've got two families.» «Yeah, kinda,» says Minnie, which is exactly right. Not every day is happy, and friends who get it don't pretend it is. And then there's Shay, who lives in a children's home. His friend Niall asks Bríd, Shay's key worker, «Can Shay come to my party? Is there, like, a form or somethin'?» Bríd laughs. «There is, but I'll sign it.» Shay is buzzing (thrilled). Niall didn't make it weird. That's the whole trick.
explain: Think back over all these families: one mum, two dads, Nana, Aunt Denise, foster mums, a key worker called Bríd, a dad in Dubai, a mam in prison, a mum who died and a dad who never calls. Every one of them is a real family, because a family is the people who look after you and love you, whatever shape they make. Some stories end happily, some don't, and most are still going. What every child needs from the people around them is the same: respect, a seat at lunch, and nobody making it weird. Be the Bilal.
fact: Friends who get it don't need to fix things; just being there helps.
fact: Knowing someone in a similar situation helps a child feel less alone.
fact: Saying good things about a friend's family makes them proud.
fact: Lots of children have separated parents, a relative in prison, or a family that looks different.
fact: Quiet help, without an audience, protects a friend's pride.
fact: Knowing a safe place to go, like a friend's house with a parent's okay, helps a child feel safe.
fact: Sharing what you can, like tea on Fridays, is part of being a good friend.
fact: Seeing adoption or foster care as normal, or even cool, helps a friend.
fact: Children in care sometimes need a permission form for parties and sleepovers; it's a small thing, and friends shouldn't make it weird.
fact: A family is the people who look after you and love you, whatever shape it has.
fact: Every family deserves respect, and not every family story has a happy ending.
fact: «No cap» means no lie, «lush» is Welsh for lovely, «inside» can mean in prison, «ta» means thanks, «tae» is Scots for to, «bussin'» means really good, and «buzzing» means thrilled.
exercise: What did Bilal do for Corey after the Father's Day joke? | Sat with him and made a joke | Made a long speech about dads | Told the joker off in front of everyone | Left him alone so he could think
exercise: Why did Corey laugh? | Bilal made it feel normal | Bilal gave him money | The joker said sorry | Bilal's dad came to school
exercise: What helped Sienna-Rae relax? | Roisin's cousin being in foster care too | Roisin telling her to forget all about it | A teacher moving her to another table | Roisin giving her the answers in a test
exercise: «The best mac and cheese, no cap.» What does «no cap» mean? | No lie | No hat | No limit | No cheese
exercise: «Two Christmases, it's well lush.» What does «lush» mean? | Lovely | Wet | Expensive | Boring
exercise: «My uncle was inside.» What does «inside» mean here? | In prison | In the house | In hospital | Indoors all day
exercise: How did Jamal feel after Kieran's story? | Less ashamed | More ashamed | Angry at Kieran | Bored
exercise: Elijah's friend says, «Your dads are so chill.» What does it do? | Makes Elijah proud of his family | Makes Elijah feel weird about his dads | Makes Elijah's dads cross with the friend | Makes the teacher send a letter home
exercise: Why did Jodie bring the jumper in a carrier bag, with no fuss? | To help Holly-Mae without embarrassing her | Because she was ashamed of her own mam | So she could tell everyone later about it | Because it was a secret present from a teacher
exercise: «Ta, pet.» What does Holly-Mae mean? | Thanks, love | Bye, dog | Take it back | Too small
exercise: Ross says Liam-Jay can stay «if ye ever need tae». What does that give Liam-Jay? | A safe place he knows he can go | A sleepover every night of the week | Ross's bedroom to keep for himself | A reason to stay away from school
exercise: What does Tom do for Kwesi? | Has him round for tea every Friday | Lends him money for trainers | Walks him home every day | Does his homework for him
exercise: Minnie's friend says, «You've got two families.» What does Minnie say? | «Yeah, kinda» | «No, just one» | «Stop it» | «I don't know»
exercise: What did Niall ask Bríd? | If Shay could come to his party, and if there was a form | If Shay could live with him and his family for good | If he could visit the children's home to play every day | Why Shay lives in a children's home and not at home
exercise: «There is, but I'll sign it.» What did Bríd mean? | There's a form, and she'd happily sign it | There's no form, so Shay can't go | Shay must sign it himself | Niall's mum must sign it
exercise: Shay is «buzzing». How does he feel? | Thrilled | Itchy | Tired | Nervous
exercise: According to the lesson, what makes a family? | The people who look after you and love you | Having exactly one mum and one dad at home | Everyone in the house sharing the same surname | Living in the same house for at least ten years
test: «if ur mate's sad about their family, u gotta say something really clever to fix it». Right? | No, just being there helps most | Yes, you must find the perfect words | Yes, or you're not a real friend | No, you should leave them alone
test: What would most likely happen if Jodie had handed over the jumper in front of the whole class? | Holly-Mae might feel embarrassed | Holly-Mae would feel even better | Everyone would ask for jumpers | Nothing different would happen
test: «Shay's in a home, so it's too much hassle to invite him». What's true? | A form is a small thing; just invite him | Kids in care are never allowed at parties | It's kinder not to invite kids in care | Only kids in care can invite kids in care
test: In a film, the new kid only makes friends after a big talent show win. What's more real? | Small things, like a seat at lunch, make friends | Only talent shows make friends in real life | Kids can't make friends without being famous | Friends always come after you win something
test: Megan says «mine split up too» to Ffion. Why does that help? | Ffion feels less alone | Ffion now has to talk about it | It makes Ffion feel worse | Megan wants to compare presents
test: Tyler: «ur gran's mac and cheese is bussin, no cap». What does Tyler think? | It's really, truly good | It's okay, nothing special | He's lying to be polite | It needs more cheese
test: ★★★ Tom has Kwesi round every Friday. 1 September is a Friday, and September has 30 days. How many Friday teas in September? | 5 | 4 | 6 | 30
test: ★★★ Niall's party is on Saturday. Bríd needs the form two days before. What is the latest day she can sign it? | Thursday | Friday | Wednesday | Saturday
test: ★★★ Roisin says, «My cousin's in foster care.» Sienna-Rae is too. Which of these must be true? | At least two children Roisin knows are in foster care | Roisin's cousin and Sienna-Rae live in the same home | Roisin is in foster care as well as her cousin | Sienna-Rae and Roisin's cousin have the same foster mum
test: ★★★ At Bilal's table: Bilal, Corey and three more kids. Two leave for football, then Kieran and Jamal sit down. How many at the table now? | 5 | 4 | 3 | 7
test: Kwesi's mum: «that boy's a good friend». Why does she think so? | He shares what he can, every week | He gives Kwesi expensive presents | He always lets Kwesi win at games | He tells Kwesi's secrets to his mum
test: «families with no mum and dad aren't proper families». Right? | No, a family is the people who look after and love you | Yes, a family needs exactly one mum and one dad | Yes, unless the court says otherwise in a letter | No, but they become proper when the child grows up
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the conduct of a friend who «gets it», as described in the lesson? | Presence and ordinary companionship, without any attempt to remedy matters | A lengthy address explaining how the friend ought to feel about the matter | The public announcement of the friend's circumstances to the class | The avoidance of the friend until the difficulty has wholly resolved itself
exam: Identify the principal advantage of rendering assistance quietly and without an audience. | It protects the dignity of the person assisted | It ensures that the helper receives due credit | It avoids any expense whatsoever to the helper | It is mandated by the rules of every school
exam: Given that a child in care requires a signed permission form to attend a party, ascertain the appropriate response of the inviting friend. | To invite the child and treat the form as a small matter | To withdraw the invitation owing to the administrative burden | To require that the child supply the form without assistance | To enquire publicly as to the reasons for the child's care
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the definition of a family. | The persons who care for and love the child, in whatever form | Exclusively a mother and a father residing together in one house | Exclusively persons sharing a common surname and place of birth | Exclusively persons related by birth and never by any court order
